item.text:
Local UCDP/SIPRI priors make this a limited-exposure case: Bangladesh had no internationalized conflict events or fatalities in 2022, and the useful Chapter 2B evidence is therefore Hasina’s diplomatic, legal, and regional-security conduct rather than wartime operations.

**Reused evidence**
None. The existing 1B nuclear sources were not materially relevant to 2B’s international-conduct question.

**New evidence**
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"2022_Statement_DPR_Ukraine Crisis.","publisher":"Permanent Mission of Bangladesh to the United Nations, New York","publication_date":"2022-03-01","url":"https://nypm.mofa.gov.bd/pages/static-pages/6952668535ce18e1c05a9c55","claim":"Bangladesh called for immediate cessation of hostilities in Ukraine, urged all parties to respect the UN Charter, and asked them to resume diplomacy and dialogue to settle disputes peacefully.","locator":"statement body","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary contemporaneous UN mission statement with explicit Bangladesh position and date.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"directly within 2022 and tied to the Ukraine war response","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to Bangladesh’s UN delegation under the Hasina government; it reflects state policy, not battlefield command.","contrary_evidence":["The statement is diplomatic and not a test of military restraint in a live war.","It does not show a Hasina-only decision distinct from the foreign ministry."],"lenses":["2B.1","2B.4","2B.6","2B.7","2B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Why didn't Bangladesh vote on UN resolution condemning Russia? Momen explains","publisher":"The Daily Star","publication_date":"2022-03-06","url":"https://www.thedailystar.net/news/world/2022-russian-invasion-ukraine/news/why-didnt-bangladesh-vote-un-resolution-condemning-russia-momen-explains-2977011","claim":"Bangladesh abstained on the first UNGA resolution condemning Russia because the foreign minister said the text was about blaming Russia rather than stopping the war, and insisted Bangladesh wanted peace and dialogue.","locator":"quoted remarks by Foreign Minister AK Abdul Momen","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous reporting with direct quotations from the foreign minister, though mediated by local press.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"directly in 2022 and relevant to Bangladesh’s initial Ukraine posture","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to the Hasina government’s foreign-policy line, though the statement is from the foreign minister rather than Hasina personally.","contrary_evidence":["Abstention is more cautious than proactive peace leadership.","The justification can be read as balancing/diplomatic hedging rather than clear norm enforcement."],"lenses":["2B.3","2B.6","2B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Bangladesh votes for UN resolution against Russia","publisher":"The Daily Star","publication_date":"2022-10-13","url":"https://online.thedailystar.net/news/world/2022-russian-invasion-ukraine/news/bangladesh-votes-un-resolution-against-russia-3141661","claim":"Bangladesh voted for the UN resolution on Ukraine’s territorial integrity and said it did so because the UN Charter’s principles on sovereignty, territorial integrity, and peaceful settlement must apply universally.","locator":"news report quoting the foreign ministry statement","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous reporting quoting an official government explanation for the vote.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"directly in 2022 and directly tied to Bangladesh’s UN vote on the war","ruler_attribution":"State policy under Hasina’s government; the vote is attributable to the government even if not personally signed by Hasina.","contrary_evidence":["This is a diplomatic vote, not evidence of operational coercive restraint.","It came after earlier abstentions, so the posture was mixed rather than uniformly hard-line."],"lenses":["2B.1","2B.6","2B.7","2B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Bangladesh abstains from UN vote against Russia","publisher":"The Daily Star","publication_date":"2022-11-15","url":"https://www.thedailystar.net/news/world/2022-russian-invasion-ukraine/news/bangladesh-abstains-unga-vote-against-russia-3170221","claim":"Bangladesh abstained on the UNGA resolution about Russian reparations for Ukraine, showing continued caution even after later 2022 pro-Ukraine voting.","locator":"opening paragraphs on the UN vote and Bangladesh’s abstention","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous reporting, but the article is summary-level and does not add ruler-specific reasoning beyond the vote itself.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"directly in 2022","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to Bangladesh’s government position under Hasina, not to a battlefield command decision.","contrary_evidence":["The abstention may reflect diplomatic hedging rather than principled restraint.","It cuts against a simple narrative of consistently aligning with anti-aggression votes."],"lenses":["2B.3","2B.6","2B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Bangladesh for expediting verification of Rohingyas for repatriation to Myanmar","publisher":"The Daily Star","publication_date":"2022-06-14","url":"https://www.thedailystar.net/news/bangladesh/news/bangladesh-expediting-verification-rohingyas-repatriation-myanmar-3047321","claim":"Bangladesh pressed Myanmar to speed Rohingya verification so repatriation could start, and said the return should ensure security, livelihood, and wellbeing for returnees.","locator":"report of the fifth Joint Working Group meeting","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous report of an official bilateral process, with concrete diplomatic claims and named officials.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"directly in 2022 and directly about a major regional-security issue","ruler_attribution":"Government diplomacy under Hasina; the claim is about bilateral policy rather than personal command authority.","contrary_evidence":["The process was still stalled after years, so the result was limited.","It shows effort, not success."],"lenses":["2B.1","2B.7","2B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"2022_Statement_DPR_Situation of Rohingya Muslims in Myanmar","publisher":"Permanent Mission of Bangladesh to the United Nations, New York","publication_date":"2022-11-16","url":"https://nypm.mofa.gov.bd/pages/static-pages/6952667135ce18e1c05a9221","claim":"Bangladesh said it had taken multi-pronged diplomatic efforts bilaterally and multilaterally to facilitate the safe, voluntary, and sustainable return of Rohingya refugees and warned the crisis could become a regional security crisis.","locator":"statement body, especially the paragraphs on diplomatic efforts and regional security risk","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary UN mission statement with clear dated policy language and explicit diplomatic aims.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"directly in 2022","ruler_attribution":"State diplomacy under Hasina’s government; attributable to the government, though not a personal military order.","contrary_evidence":["Again, this is a process statement, not proof of successful repatriation.","It reflects humanitarian/diplomatic handling more than a direct aggression decision."],"lenses":["2B.7","2B.9","2B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"India - Bangladesh Joint Statement during the State Visit of Prime Minister of Bangladesh to India","publisher":"Press Information Bureau, Government of India","publication_date":"2022-09-07","url":"https://www.pib.gov.in/Pressreleaseshare.aspx?PRID=1857392&lang=2&reg=48","claim":"During Hasina’s September 2022 state visit, the two prime ministers discussed political and security cooperation, border management, trade, connectivity, water resources, cyber security, and agreed the bilateral relationship rested on sovereignty, equality, trust, and understanding.","locator":"paragraphs 2 and 5 of the joint statement","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official joint statement from the host government with specific agenda items and date.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"directly in 2022 and directly tied to Hasina’s external conduct","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Hasina as the visiting head of government.","contrary_evidence":["A broad cooperation statement does not itself prove peace leadership.","It is a bilateral diplomatic event, not conflict de-escalation in a war zone."],"lenses":["2B.1","2B.7","2B.8","2B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"English Translation of Press Statement by Prime Minister Shri Narendra Modi during the visit of Prime Minister of Bangladesh to India","publisher":"Press Information Bureau, Government of India","publication_date":"2022-09-06","url":"https://www.pib.gov.in/PressReleasePage.aspx?PRID=1857166","claim":"Modi said India and Bangladesh signed a Kushiyara river water-sharing agreement and discussed flood mitigation and cooperation against terrorism and radicalism during Hasina’s visit.","locator":"press statement paragraphs on the Kushiyara agreement, flood mitigation, and anti-terror cooperation","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official government press statement with specific signed agreement and meeting themes.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"directly in 2022","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to Hasina’s state visit and bilateral diplomacy, though the wording is from the Indian side.","contrary_evidence":["This is the Indian prime minister’s statement, so it is an external corroboration rather than a Bangladesh-authored record.","It does not directly evaluate Hasina’s peace conduct beyond the diplomatic agenda."],"lenses":["2B.1","2B.7","2B.8","2B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Cabinet approves Memorandum of Understanding between India and Bangladesh on withdrawal of upto 153 cusecs of water each by India and Bangladesh from common border river Kushiyara","publisher":"Press Information Bureau, Government of India","publication_date":"2022-09-28","url":"https://www.pib.gov.in/Pressreleaseshare.aspx?PRID=1862940&lang=2&reg=48","claim":"India and Bangladesh signed the Kushiyara water-sharing MoU on 6 September 2022, creating a joint monitoring team and formalizing a cooperative border-river arrangement.","locator":"body text describing the signature date and monitoring mechanism","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Specific treaty-like bilateral arrangement in an official government release with a concrete implementation mechanism.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"directly in 2022","ruler_attribution":"Part of Hasina’s external diplomacy because the MoU was signed during her state visit, though the document itself is intergovernmental.","contrary_evidence":["Water-sharing is a cooperation file, not a direct test of war/peace in a conflict.","It shows lawful management of shared resources rather than a broader strategic settlement."],"lenses":["2B.1","2B.7","2B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"18th Meeting of the Joint Working Group (JWG) on Security and Border Management between India and Bangladesh held","publisher":"Press Information Bureau, Government of India","publication_date":"2022-12-06","url":"https://www.pib.gov.in/Pressreleaseshare.aspx?PRID=1881259&lang=2&reg=48","claim":"India and Bangladesh reiterated commitment to deepen cooperation on border and security issues, including border fencing, illegal crossing, insurgency, terrorism, organized crime, and smuggling.","locator":"paragraphs describing the meeting agenda and bilateral commitments","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official government summary of a bilateral security-management meeting with concrete agenda items.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"directly in 2022","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to the Hasina government’s border-management posture, though not a personal operational order.","contrary_evidence":["Border-management cooperation is partly routine and not a special peace initiative.","This is bilateral security administration rather than conflict settlement."],"lenses":["2B.7","2B.8","2B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Bangladesh votes for UN resolution against Russia / Bangladesh abstains on related vote in March 2022","publisher":"The Daily Star / bdnews24.com","publication_date":"2022-03-05 to 2022-10-13","url":"https://www.thedailystar.net/news/world/2022-russian-invasion-ukraine/news/why-didnt-bangladesh-vote-un-resolution-condemning-russia-2977011","claim":"Across March and October 2022, Bangladesh’s Ukraine position combined an initial abstention justified in peace terms with a later vote explicitly supporting sovereignty and territorial integrity.","locator":"comparison of the March abstention reporting and the October affirmative vote reporting","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Two contemporaneous reporting strands together show the mixed but non-aggressive posture more clearly than either one alone.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"directly in 2022","ruler_attribution":"Government foreign policy under Hasina, with no evidence of any personal escalation order.","contrary_evidence":["The mixture of abstention and support weakens any claim of fully consistent anti-aggression leadership.","It still does not amount to direct operational conflict conduct."],"lenses":["2B.1","2B.3","2B.6","2B.10"]}

**Search and rejection summary**
- I considered roughly 25-40 documents across UN mission pages, UN press material, Bangladesh and Indian government releases, Reuters-linked coverage, AP, Euronews, The Daily Star, bdnews24, Prothom Alo, Dhaka Tribune, and a few broader regional-security pages.
- I rejected generic peacekeeping and development pages that did not bear on 2022 external conduct.
- I rejected most nuclear and civil-safety material from the earlier chapter because 2B is about international peace, not existential-risk responsibility.
- I also rejected several duplicate items that repeated the same Ukraine vote, same Rohingya repatriation point, or same India visit without adding a materially distinct claim.
- Reuters text hits were limited in this pass; where Reuters was only available via photo or syndicated image metadata, I preferred official releases or direct reporting that added a concrete claim.
- I did not use the client matrix, and I did not re-fetch the local UCDP/SIPRI structured priors because they were already supplied.

**Remaining gaps**
- There is still no evidence of a Bangladesh-initiated interstate war, proxy war, annexation, or cross-border coercion in 2022.
- The record is strong on diplomacy and regional management, but weak on any Hasina-specific peace bargain with a live adversary or any direct accountability for military conduct, because there was no major external war.
- If you want a tighter 2B dossier, the next useful search would be around any 2022 Bangladesh-Myanmar or Bangladesh-India security incidents that show Hasina personally authorizing restraint, de-escalation, or accountability beyond the routine diplomatic record.
